import{_ as t,o as s,c as a,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"External work in the project views","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/external-work-episode.md","filePath":"records/external-work-episode.md"}'),n={name:"records/external-work-episode.md"};function r(i,e,d,l,c,h){return s(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="external-work-in-the-project-views" tabindex="-1">External work in the project views <a class="header-anchor" href="#external-work-in-the-project-views" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;External work in the project views&quot;">​</a></h1><p>2026-11-08. The person ran a small mean-comparison task in DeepSeek Harness, opened its Rho project and found an empty Conversations page, Graph and Console. The shared Notebook had the two real cells and their values, but their workspace held only a figure. They asked for the same Rho experience whichever window held the Agent&#39;s conversation.</p><h2 id="what-the-run-showed" tabindex="-1">What the run showed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-run-showed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the run showed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The project contains six synthetic values: Control 1, 2, 3 and Treatment 2, 4, 6. Harness had already computed means 2 and 4, written a script, CSV and PNG, and drawn a second plot in the shared R session. Its native session has two turns and 191 committed events. No analysis was repeated for this investigation.</p><p>There were two independent gaps:</p><ul><li>The setup used a plain MCP entry. It supplied project tools but did not configure native conversation observation. The installed Harness was now 0.2.2-rc.1; its installer refused dsh-rho 0.1.5, which declared only 0.2.1-rc.5. A connection grant is not a conversation record.</li><li>Rho&#39;s process readers consumed <code>conversation.read</code> for sessions started in Rho. External sessions had their own list and detail view, but the Graph, Console and derived Studio did not read that record. The Graph also returned its empty state whenever <code>snapshot.conversations</code> was empty, even with real Notebook events.</li></ul><p>The original Harness version-4 log was read through the Agent owner&#39;s public projection, retaining its original session, turn and tool-call identities. This recovered the existing record without starting an Agent, a Core Host or another R session. dsh-rho was then configured with the existing grant for this exact folder. The redundant global MCP entry was disabled after the scoped bridge worked.</p><h2 id="changes-and-observed-results" tabindex="-1">Changes and observed results <a class="header-anchor" href="#changes-and-observed-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changes and observed results&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>src/external-work.ts</code> projects the public <code>agent.sessions.read</code> result into the same presentation used by Rho conversations. <code>web/work-turns.ts</code> reads both sources for the Graph, Console, answer board and one session&#39;s Studio. The shared native index is polled every three seconds; at most six sessions and eight recent turns per session are read for the project views. A Studio reads its selected session. The Graph retains its 160-node drawing limit. Earlier content omitted by the owners remains omitted; these reads do not recover it.</p><p>The original session now has one Conversations row. Its Studio is listed under Studio and lays out the input, script, Commands/Notebook tabs, figure and summary table. The person&#39;s existing single-figure workspace remains their arrangement. The default input pane was too short to expose its table rows after the file description and controls; the three process panes now give inputs and Notebook more height. Saved personal layouts retain their own proportions. Opening an external session&#39;s Studio keeps the Agent panel optional; the native conversation is available from the Agent page and panel under the same identity.</p><p>The Graph draws two recorded questions, six files and fourteen shell commands. Its timeline joins the two actual Notebook cells by the cell IDs in their tool replies. A project grant can serve several native sessions, so it is not used to assign every cell to one conversation. The notes&#39; undated meaning row remains available separately: it no longer counts as another native conversation, adds a pause from 1970 or takes the opening viewport away from the recorded work.</p><p>The Studio Notebook initially remained empty because its scope knew only a Rho-started conversation&#39;s principal. Kernel 0.5.10 accepts exact cell IDs from the retained native receipts. The same two original cells now appear in this session&#39;s Studio. A focused owner check rejects another conversation&#39;s cell even when it shares both the grant and the R session. The project Notebook still shows the full project session. Its cell filter does not reconstruct earlier output states.</p><p>The Console shows the fourteen native shell commands, including two nonzero exits and their retained output. Run again was checked: it filled a command draft and required Run; the draft was cleared and no command was dispatched. Notebook cells remain in Notebook, including its Console form. A shell-command empty state now explains that distinction. Tables 0.5.1 labels selection feedback for the Agent.</p><p>The external answer board shows the reported result links and offers the same session&#39;s conversation. Rho-owned answer decisions and direct follow-up forms are kept on the records that own those operations; the native record is not submitted as a fabricated Rho conversation.</p><h2 id="times-versions-and-uncertainty" tabindex="-1">Times, versions and uncertainty <a class="header-anchor" href="#times-versions-and-uncertainty" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Times, versions and uncertainty&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A native answer&#39;s file link is labelled reported, not a disk write observation. It can open the current file; only an exact project/resource/version tuple in a retained receipt supplies a historical version. Today&#39;s file modification time is not reconstructed as a past observation. Studio&#39;s current captures are labelled as current and are not added retrospectively to the native record.</p><p>The older native projection retains a tool&#39;s call time but not its result time. It therefore supplies no invented command duration. A replay before the cut that retained a result hides that output and says its outcome was unknown then. The Agent panel&#39;s native messages obey the same cut. Failed tool results retain failure without inventing an exit code; unreported exits and missing bytes remain unknown.</p><p>The live R session stayed idle with the same two cell IDs and four values. The native session still had two turns and source cursor 191; the CSV, script and PNG were read, not regenerated. The WebUI was built in an isolated checkout with its selected owners and exercised through a loopback preview forwarding API requests to the existing service. The checked static WebUI assets were then installed into the source-linked application&#39;s existing browser directory, retaining older assets and a rollback copy and replacing the index last. The original service address now serves those same checked bytes. Neither the service nor its R session was restarted; this is an explicit local browser-build override, not an npm application upgrade.</p><h2 id="harness-compatibility-and-recovery" tabindex="-1">Harness compatibility and recovery <a class="header-anchor" href="#harness-compatibility-and-recovery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Harness compatibility and recovery&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The first 0.1.5 installation attempt was refused by Harness&#39;s compatibility check. The installed 0.2.2-rc.1 package and its public documentation retain <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/master/docs/subsystems/session-query.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>sessionQuery.observeSession</code></a>, its disposable observation lease and <code>listSessions</code>. dsh-rho 0.1.6 declares the two checked Harness versions and reads a cold session&#39;s header through that lease, removing its dependency on <code>sessionController.inspect</code>. Both header and capture leases are disposed. No version exemption was used.</p><p>The locally packed 0.1.6 installed normally in the actual Desktop app. Its Rho entry verified the matching folder, tools and observed conversation. This checks the current Desktop integration; it does not establish other Harness versions or other operating systems.</p><p>A read outage was injected only in the owned preview. The first run misleadingly said every conversation was in the trash; that fallback was removed. The repeated run showed the read error, kept the input disabled until its session was known, and recovered the same row without a reload or message. The Graph and Studio also distinguish a failed read from an empty history.</p><h2 id="verification-and-scope" tabindex="-1">Verification and scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification-and-scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification and scope&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The service and WebUI builds and contract check passed. Focused checks cover native identity and project containment, exact versus reported versions, replay cuts, failed tools, session deduplication, Notebook event attribution, Studio roles, existing conversation continuation, conditional layouts and decisions. The adapter suite also checks a cold read without starting an Agent. Real browser checks cover the existing session, its work views, a failed command&#39;s output, preparing and clearing a rerun draft, reading failure and recovery, and layouts at CSS widths 1440, 1024, 768 and 392 pixels. No horizontal page overflow was observed; narrow Studio views use their tab strip and retain the two cells and plot.</p><p>Screenshots and bounded API observations are retained locally. This is functional and visual evidence for the Harness episode, not an independent usability study. Claude Code and Codex use the same public native-session presentation, but a new real ChatGPT or Codex-client run was not part of this episode. Source delivery, the local browser-build update, an npm release and replacement of the resident backend remain separate outcomes.</p>',26)])])}const w=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,w as default};
